/* ============================================================
   CASE STUDY 01 — HRM GENIE
   ============================================================ */
function CS_HRM(){
  return `
<header class="cs-hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <a href="#/work" class="cs-back">${I.arL} All case studies</a>
    <p class="eyebrow rv" style="margin-bottom:20px"><span class="n">01</span> &nbsp;—&nbsp; Enterprise HRMS · 0→1 · Product Designer + Product Manager</p>
    <h1 class="h1 cs-title rv" style="--i:1">HRM Genie — <span class="em" style="color:var(--fg-2)">making policy enforce itself</span></h1>
    <p class="lead cs-sub rv" style="--i:2">
      A twelve-module HR platform for a 1,000-person organisation. The features were never the hard part.
      The hard part was encoding real employment policy — with all its exceptions — into rules that an
      HR admin could configure without breaking payroll for everyone.
    </p>
    <div class="cs-facts rv" style="--i:3">
      ${[
        {b:'Designer + PM',s:'Owned both the roadmap and the design'},
        {b:'12 modules',s:'Shipped end to end'},
        {b:'32 people',s:'8 HR managers, 24 employees interviewed'},
        {b:'12 participants',s:'Moderated usability testing'}
      ].map(f=>`<div class="cs-fact"><b>${f.b}</b><span>${f.s}</span></div>`).join('')}
    </div>
    ${roleNote(`I held both roles on this product. As <strong>PM</strong> I ran discovery, wrote the requirements, sequenced twelve modules into a release order, and made the scope trade-offs with engineering. As <strong>designer</strong> I did the research, the information architecture, the flows and the UI — then shipped a chunk of the front-end in code.`,
      `The reason this matters for a case study: <span class="hl">most of the decisions below are product decisions expressed as interface decisions.</span> Version-first policy architecture is a data-model call. Sandwich-leave detection as a per-type toggle is a scope call. I could make them because I owned both sides.`)}
    <div class="rv" style="--i:4">${ndaNote}</div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('01','The problem','HR was managing people with tools built for filing cabinets')}
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="rv">
        <blockquote class="quote">
          <p>"I have five spreadsheets open just to approve one leave request. I have to check the policy, the calendar, the balance, the org chart — none of them talk to each other."</p>
          <cite>HR Manager — discovery interview, one of eight</cite>
        </blockquote>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-4 rv" style="--i:1">
        <p class="body">
          Discovery surfaced three compounding failures. Leave requests died in email threads.
          Payroll had to be manually cross-checked against attendance every month. Recruitment had
          no single source of truth.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          But the finding that actually shaped the product was subtler: <span class="hl">every one of these
          organisations had policies that differed from the textbook</span>. Sandwich leave applied to
          some leave types and not others. Grace periods had tiers, not a binary. Approval chains ran
          four to five levels deep and varied by department.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          Off-the-shelf HRMS tools ship fixed rules. That's why these teams were back in spreadsheets —
          <span class="hl">not because the software lacked features, but because it couldn't express their policy.</span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mets rv" style="margin-top:clamp(36px,5vw,60px)">
      ${[
        {b:'~6 hrs',l:'Lost per week per HR manager',m:'Self-reported in discovery interviews, averaged across 8 participants'},
        {b:'4 tools',l:'Zero integration between them',m:'Attendance, leave, payroll and recruitment lived in separate systems'},
        {b:'Every cycle',l:'Manual payroll cross-check',m:'Attendance data reconciled by hand against payroll before every run'}
      ].map(m=>`<div class="met"><b>${m.b}</b><span class="ml">${m.l}</span><span class="mm">${m.m}</span></div>`).join('')}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('02','Design decisions','Three decisions that carried the product','Twelve modules shipped. These three are the ones where the first answer was wrong, and finding the second one is what the job actually was.')}

    <!-- DECISION 1 -->
    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 01 · Leave management</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">Show the cost of a decision before it's made, not after</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        Sandwich leave is a policy where taking leave either side of a weekend deducts the weekend too.
        Apply for Friday and Monday, lose four days, not two. It's standard in Indian employment policy
        and it is universally misunderstood by employees.
      </p>

      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">What I shipped first</span>
          <p>A warning on the confirmation screen, after the employee picked their dates. Technically accurate. Correctly worded.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">What broke</span>
          <p>Employees submitted anyway, then disputed the deduction after approval. The warning arrived after they'd already committed — reversing it meant unwinding an approved request through HR.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat good">
          <span class="lbl">What shipped</span>
          <p>A live day counter inside the date picker itself. The number updates as you select. Sandwich days highlight in the calendar in a distinct state. You see four days before you've decided on two.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="note rv" style="margin-bottom:30px">
        <span class="lbl">Why this matters beyond leave</span>
        <p>A warning placed after commitment isn't a warning — it's an explanation. Any interface where the system's arithmetic differs from the user's mental model has this problem, and the fix is almost always to move the truth earlier, into the moment of choice.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- DEMO -->
      <div class="panel rv" id="demo-leave">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">HRM Genie — apply for leave</span>
          <span class="live"><i></i> Interactive</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <div class="grid-2 tight">
            <div>
              <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;gap:12px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:16px">
                <p class="small" style="color:var(--fg-2);margin:0"><strong>April 2026</strong></p>
                <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Leave type">
                  <button data-lt="casual" aria-pressed="true">Casual</button>
                  <button data-lt="earned" aria-pressed="false">Earned</button>
                  <button data-lt="sick" aria-pressed="false">Sick</button>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="cal" id="cal" role="group" aria-label="Select leave dates"></div>
              <p class="small" style="margin-top:14px;color:var(--fg-4);font-size:.76rem">
                Click dates to select. Try <strong style="color:var(--fg-3)">Friday 3rd</strong> and <strong style="color:var(--fg-3)">Monday 6th</strong>.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:12px">Deduction</p>
              <div class="tally">
                <b id="tally-n">0</b>
                <span id="tally-l">days selected</span>
              </div>
              <div class="alert hide" id="sw-alert">
                ${I.warn}
                <span>Sandwich leave applies. <b id="sw-detail"></b></span>
              </div>
              <div style="margin-top:26px">
                <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;margin-bottom:14px">
                  <p class="eyebrow" style="margin:0">Balance after this request</p>
                </div>
                <div class="bal" id="bal"></div>
              </div>
              <div style="margin-top:24px;display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap">
                <button class="btn btn-p" style="font-size:.83rem;padding:9px 16px" id="lv-submit">Submit request</button>
                <button class="btn btn-s" style="font-size:.83rem;padding:9px 16px" id="lv-reset">Clear</button>
              </div>
              <p class="small" id="lv-msg" style="margin-top:12px;min-height:20px;color:var(--ok);font-size:.8rem"></p>
              <div style="margin-top:22px;padding-top:22px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
                <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:14px">Approval chain — routed from org hierarchy</p>
                <div class="chain" id="chain">
                  <div class="chain-s done"><span class="chain-d"></span><b>Arjun R.</b><span>You — applied</span></div>
                  <div class="chain-s" id="ch-1"><span class="chain-d"></span><b>Ravi S.</b><span>L1 — Manager</span></div>
                  <div class="chain-s" id="ch-2"><span class="chain-d"></span><b>Priya M.</b><span>L2 — HR Head</span></div>
                  <div class="chain-s" id="ch-3"><span class="chain-d"></span><b>Balance</b><span>Auto-updates</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="small rv" style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--fg-4);font-size:.76rem">
        Sandwich detection is configurable per leave type — enabled on Casual, disabled on Earned and Sick.
        Switch the type above and reselect to see the rule change.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- DECISION 2 -->
    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 02 · Policy management</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">Versions, not edits — so acknowledgement can't go stale</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        Organisations update HR policy constantly. Employees ignore the emails. Every HR team I
        interviewed had the same unanswerable question during audits: <span class="hl">who has actually
        read the current version of this?</span>
      </p>
      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">The obvious model</span>
          <p>A policy document with an edit button and an "acknowledged" checkbox per employee. This is what every competitor ships.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">Why it fails silently</span>
          <p>Edit the policy and the acknowledgement stays ticked. The record now says 142 employees agreed to a document that no longer exists. It looks like compliance and is the opposite.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat good">
          <span class="lbl">What shipped</span>
          <p>Every edit creates a new version rather than overwriting. Acknowledgement is bound to a version, so publishing v3.1 resets state and re-prompts. HR can query who acknowledged v1 versus v2 at any point in time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">
        <span class="lbl">The design decision underneath</span>
        <p>This is a data-model decision that presents as a UI decision. I could not have specified it without understanding how the acknowledgement record would be queried during an audit — which is why I map rules before I draw screens.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel rv" style="margin-top:30px" id="demo-policy">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">HRM Genie — policy versioning</span>
          <span class="live"><i></i> Interactive</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <div class="grid-2 tight">
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:14px">Code of conduct</p>
              <div style="display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:10px;margin-bottom:6px">
                <p class="h3" id="pol-v" style="font-size:1.5rem">v3.1</p>
                <span class="work-tag" style="border-color:var(--ac-line);color:var(--ac)">Current</span>
              </div>
              <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:22px">Published 2 Jan 2026 by Priya M., HR Admin</p>
              <button class="btn btn-s" style="font-size:.83rem;padding:9px 16px" id="pol-publish">Publish new version</button>
              <p class="small" style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--fg-4);font-size:.76rem">
                Watch what happens to acknowledgement state when you publish.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:16px">Acknowledgement — 142 employees</p>
              <div class="comp">
                <div class="comp-r">
                  <span class="nm">Acknowledged</span>
                  <span class="comp-tr"><span class="comp-f" id="ack-f" style="width:83%;background:var(--ok)"></span></span>
                  <span class="vl" id="ack-v" style="color:var(--ok)">83%</span>
                </div>
                <div class="comp-r">
                  <span class="nm">Pending</span>
                  <span class="comp-tr"><span class="comp-f" id="pen-f" style="width:17%;background:var(--warn)"></span></span>
                  <span class="vl" id="pen-v" style="color:var(--warn)">17%</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="callout" style="margin-top:22px;font-size:.8rem" id="pol-log">
                Steady state — 118 of 142 employees have acknowledged v3.1.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- DECISION 3 -->
    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 03 · Attendance</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">Give the dispute to the person who has the evidence</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        Biometric and web check-in both feed one attendance log, with a configurable grace period that
        classifies lateness into tiers rather than a binary. The interesting problem wasn't capture —
        it was what happens when the log is wrong.
      </p>
      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">The default</span>
          <p>Employee emails HR. HR checks the device log, corrects the record manually, replies. Roughly two hours a week of HR time in a 1,000-person org.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">The real asymmetry</span>
          <p>HR has the system access but the employee has the evidence — they know they badged in at 9:02 and the reader failed. Routing through HR puts the decision furthest from the facts.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat good">
          <span class="lbl">What shipped</span>
          <p>Employees file the correction themselves with actual time and reason. HR gets a reviewable queue with bulk approve and date-range filters — added in round two after watching managers clear disputes one at a time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Honest note on impact —</strong> HR reported this cut correction handling from roughly two hours weekly to under twenty minutes. That's their number, self-reported, not an instrumented measurement. I'm citing it as what it is.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- DECISION 4 — APPROVAL CHAINS -->
    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 04 · Approval chains</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">Encode the policy once, so nobody has to remember it</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        Approval routing was the requirement that looked trivial and turned out to be the hardest thing
        in the product. Every organisation I interviewed described their chain as "manager, then HR" —
        and every one of them was wrong about their own process.
      </p>
      <p class="body read" style="margin-top:14px">
        Short leaves skipped HR entirely. Anything over a week went to a department head nobody had
        mentioned. Notice-period exceptions had a separate path. <span class="hl">The policy existed, but it
        lived in people's heads, and it only surfaced when someone routed a request wrongly.</span>
      </p>

      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">Requirement as written</span>
          <p>"Two-level approval: manager, then HR." What every stakeholder told me in the first workshop, unprompted and confidently.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">What workflow shadowing found</span>
          <p>At least four distinct routes depending on duration, leave type and department. A fixed two-level chain would have been wrong for roughly half of all real requests.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat good">
          <span class="lbl">What shipped</span>
          <p>A rule builder. HR defines conditions — duration thresholds, leave type, department — and the system composes the chain per request at submission time. One configuration, every route.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="note" style="margin-bottom:30px">
        <span class="lbl">This is where the PM half of the role earned its keep</span>
        <p>Building a rule engine instead of a fixed chain roughly tripled the engineering estimate for this module. I made that call, defended it in planning, and traded away two lower-value features to fund it — because a fixed chain would have pushed every exception back into email, which was the entire problem we were solving.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel rv" id="demo-chain">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">HRM Genie — approval rule builder</span>
          <span class="live"><i></i> Interactive</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <div class="grid-2 tight">
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:16px">Configure the rule — HR admin view</p>
              <div class="rule-set" id="rule-set"></div>
              <div class="callout" style="margin-top:20px;font-size:.8rem">
                Rules evaluate top-down. The first matching condition determines the chain, so ordering is itself a policy decision — which is why HR can reorder them.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:16px">Preview — what an employee's request would do</p>
              <div class="sim-row">
                <label class="sim-l" for="sim-days">Leave duration</label>
                <input type="range" id="sim-days" min="1" max="20" value="2" class="rng" aria-label="Leave duration in days">
                <span class="sim-v" id="sim-days-v">2 days</span>
              </div>
              <div class="sim-row" style="margin-top:14px">
                <label class="sim-l" for="sim-dept">Department</label>
                <select id="sim-dept" class="sel">
                  <option value="eng">Engineering</option>
                  <option value="sales">Sales</option>
                  <option value="hr">HR &amp; Admin</option>
                </select>
              </div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin:26px 0 14px">Resulting chain</p>
              <div class="chain" id="sim-chain"></div>
              <div class="callout" style="margin-top:18px;font-size:.8rem" id="sim-why"></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="small rv" style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--fg-4);font-size:.76rem">
        Drag the duration slider past 5 and past 10 days, and switch department, to see the chain recompose.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- DECISION 5 — RECRUITMENT -->
    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 05 · AI recruitment</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">Nobody rejects a candidate on a number they can't interrogate</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        Three AI features work together: generate a job description, parse incoming resumes, then rank
        candidates by fit. The engineering was straightforward. <span class="hl">Getting anyone to actually
        use the ranking was not.</span>
      </p>
      <p class="body read" style="margin-top:14px">
        In the first round of testing, HR managers looked at a 94% match score and then opened the resume
        and read it top to bottom anyway. The feature saved nobody any time. When I asked why, the answer
        was consistent: <em>"I don't know what it's counting."</em>
      </p>

      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">v1 — a score</span>
          <p>Candidates ranked with a single percentage. Clean, confident, and completely opaque. Zero behaviour change — reviewers read every resume in full regardless.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">The real objection</span>
          <p>Not accuracy — accountability. A hiring decision has consequences, and no manager will let an unexplained number carry any of the weight for one.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat good">
          <span class="lbl">v2 — a decomposed, editable score</span>
          <p>Every score breaks into the skills it matched and — more importantly — the ones it didn't. Requirements are editable, so the ranking recomputes live. Low-confidence extractions are flagged rather than silently included.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="note" style="margin-bottom:30px">
        <span class="lbl">The generalisable principle</span>
        <p>An AI output that can't be interrogated will be ignored, no matter how accurate it is. The design work in AI features is almost never the model — it's building the affordances that let a user hold the output accountable. <span class="hl">Show the components, show the gaps, and make the inputs editable.</span></p>
      </div>

      <p class="body read" style="margin-bottom:22px">
        The ranking is the third step. Before it there's job description generation and resume parsing —
        and each hands its output to the next, which means
        <span class="hl">an error at step one is invisible by step three.</span> Run the pipeline below
        and watch where a human is required to intervene.
      </p>

      <div class="panel rv" id="demo-pipeline" style="margin-bottom:30px">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">HRM Genie — AI recruitment pipeline</span>
          <span class="live"><i></i> Interactive</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <div class="rec-nav" id="rec-nav"></div>
          <div class="rec-stage" id="rec-stage"></div>
          <div style="display:flex;gap:10px;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;margin-top:20px">
            <button class="btn btn-p" style="font-size:.83rem;padding:9px 16px" id="rec-run">Run the pipeline</button>
            <button class="btn btn-s" style="font-size:.83rem;padding:9px 16px" id="rec-reset">Reset</button>
            <span class="small" id="rec-status" style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--fg-3)"></span>
          </div>
          <div class="callout" style="margin-top:18px;font-size:.82rem" id="rec-note">
            Four stages, each feeding the next. Press run — the interesting moment is stage two.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="panel rv" id="demo-hire">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">HRM Genie — AI candidate matching</span>
          <span class="live"><i></i> Interactive</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <div class="grid-2 tight">
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:8px">Senior Product Designer · Engineering</p>
              <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:18px">Toggle a requirement off and the ranking recomputes. HR controls the criteria, not the model.</p>
              <div class="skills" id="skills"></div>
              <div class="callout" style="margin-top:20px;font-size:.8rem">
                <strong>Weighting is visible too.</strong> Required skills count double against optional ones — shown, not hidden, so a reviewer can see why one candidate outranks another.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:16px">Ranked candidates — <span id="cand-count"></span></p>
              <div class="cands" id="cands"></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- DECISION 6 — CHATBOT -->
    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 06 · HR chatbot</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">Design the failure state first — it's the only one that matters</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        The chatbot handles the twelve queries HR answered most often: leave balances, payslips, policy
        lookups, applying for leave. Scoping it to twelve was a deliberate product decision, not a
        technical limit — a narrow bot that always works beats a broad one that sometimes doesn't.
      </p>
      <p class="body read" style="margin-top:14px">
        The design problem wasn't the twelve. <span class="hl">It was the thirteenth.</span>
      </p>

      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">What v1 did on failure</span>
          <p>"Sorry, I didn't understand that." Standard, polite, and fatal — users didn't rephrase, they abandoned the bot entirely and never came back.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">What that cost</span>
          <p>Trust is not query-scoped. One dead end taught the user the whole tool was unreliable, and they went back to emailing HR for the queries it handled perfectly well.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat good">
          <span class="lbl">What shipped</span>
          <p>Every unresolved query returns a handoff card: what it understood, what it couldn't do, who owns that request, and a one-tap route to them with context attached. The bot never says "I don't know" without saying "here's who does."</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="panel rv" id="demo-bot">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">Genie — HR assistant</span>
          <span class="live"><i></i> Interactive</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <div class="chat" id="chat"></div>
          <div class="chat-sug" id="chat-sug"></div>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:16px;color:var(--fg-4);font-size:.76rem">
            Try the last one — it's outside the twelve supported intents, and it's the interaction I care most about.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- DECISION 7 — PAYROLL -->
    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 07 · Payroll</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">The highest-impact decision was removing a screen, not adding one</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        Payroll was where every other module's data had to arrive correct. Attendance, leave, comp-off,
        reimbursements — all of it lands in one payslip once a month, and getting it wrong is the single
        most visible failure an HR system can have.
      </p>
      <p class="body read" style="margin-top:14px">
        The original process had HR manually cross-checking attendance against payroll every cycle.
        <span class="hl">The fix wasn't a better reconciliation interface — it was making reconciliation
        unnecessary</span> by having payroll read attendance directly, with every figure traceable back to
        the record that produced it.
      </p>

      <div class="panel rv" style="margin-top:30px">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">HRM Genie — payslip, April 2026</span>
          <span class="live" style="color:var(--fg-3)"><i style="background:var(--fg-3)"></i> Recreation</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <div class="grid-2 tight">
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:16px">Earnings</p>
              <div class="pay">
                ${[
                  ['Basic salary','₹45,000',''],
                  ['House rent allowance','₹18,000',''],
                  ['Special allowance','₹4,000',''],
                  ['Travel reimbursement','₹3,200','from 2 approved claims'],
                  ['Mobile reimbursement','₹800','from 1 approved claim']
                ].map(([a,b,c])=>`<div class="pay-r"><span class="pay-l">${a}${c?`<em>${c}</em>`:''}</span><span class="pay-v">${b}</span></div>`).join('')}
                <div class="pay-r total"><span class="pay-l">Gross</span><span class="pay-v">₹71,000</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:16px">Deductions</p>
              <div class="pay">
                ${[
                  ['Provident fund (12%)','−₹5,400',''],
                  ['Professional tax','−₹200',''],
                  ['Tax deducted at source','−₹2,100',''],
                  ['Employee state insurance','−₹300',''],
                  ['Loss of pay','Nil','0 unapproved absences — from attendance log']
                ].map(([a,b,c])=>`<div class="pay-r"><span class="pay-l">${a}${c?`<em>${c}</em>`:''}</span><span class="pay-v">${b}</span></div>`).join('')}
                <div class="pay-r total"><span class="pay-l">Net pay</span><span class="pay-v" style="color:var(--ok)">₹63,000</span></div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="callout" style="margin-top:22px;font-size:.82rem">
            <strong>Every derived figure names its source.</strong> Reimbursements cite the approved claims. Loss of pay cites the attendance log. A payslip query becomes a two-second lookup instead of a reconciliation exercise — which is what actually removed the manual cross-check.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('03','System scope','The other nine modules','Shipped, but conventional. Included as evidence of scale rather than narrative — I would rather show you three decisions properly than twelve superficially.')}
    <div class="scope rv">
      ${[
        {t:'Payroll',d:'Auto-imports attendance, leave and reimbursements. Removes the manual cross-check entirely'},
        {t:'AI recruitment',d:'JD generation, resume parsing, skill-match ranking — all editable, with confidence shown'},
        {t:'Performance reviews',d:'Multi-level KPI cycles with first-class draft and partial-save states'},
        {t:'Approval chains',d:'Four-to-five level routing, configured once against org hierarchy'},
        {t:'HR chatbot',d:'Top 12 recurring queries, with an explicit human handoff for every unresolved intent'},
        {t:'Email templates',d:'Token-based templates HR edits without a developer'},
        {t:'Employee directory',d:'Org chart, reporting lines, role-based visibility'},
        {t:'Holiday calendar',d:'Regional calendars with per-location configuration'},
        {t:'Reimbursements',d:'Travel and mobile claims flowing into the payslip'}
      ].map(s=>`<div class="scope-i"><b>${s.t}</b><span>${s.d}</span></div>`).join('')}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('04','Outcomes','What the design actually changed')}
    <div class="mets rv">
      ${[
        {b:'12 modules',l:'Designed and shipped end to end',m:'From discovery through dev handoff and post-launch iteration'},
        {b:'1,000+',l:'Employees on the live system',m:'Deployed across the full organisation'},
        {b:'Zero',l:'Manual payroll cross-checks',m:'Attendance auto-import removed the reconciliation step that caused correction errors'},
        {b:'~2 hrs → ~20 min',l:'Weekly attendance correction handling',m:'HR self-reported, post-launch. Not instrumented — cited as their estimate'}
      ].map(m=>`<div class="met"><b>${m.b}</b><span class="ml">${m.l}</span><span class="mm">${m.m}</span></div>`).join('')}
    </div>
    <div class="callout rv" style="margin-top:24px">
      <strong>On measurement —</strong> I've listed only what I can source and explain. Where a number came from a
      stakeholder's estimate rather than instrumentation, I've said so, because I'd rather defend four
      honest numbers than present ten I can't stand behind in a conversation.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('05','Reflection','What I got wrong')}
    <div class="grid-2">
      ${[
        {t:'I treated admin configuration as a settings dump',b:`I designed elegant employee-facing flows and gave the HR admin config screens a fraction of the attention. HR admins are not developers — they were the users who struggled most in testing. I rebuilt those screens in round two. It's the most useful lesson this project taught me and it now shapes how I scope enterprise work: <span class="hl">the admin surface is a product, not a preference pane.</span>`},
        {t:'I over-trusted acknowledgement as a compliance signal',b:`Employees with ten-plus policies queued treated acknowledgement as a dismiss button. Technically compliant, practically meaningless. I redesigned it into a prioritised must-read list with low-priority updates batched into a weekly digest — but I should have anticipated that volume creates its own failure mode.`},
        {t:'AI outputs needed visible uncertainty before anyone would use them',b:`The recruitment features tested badly until every AI output carried an explicit confidence indicator and an override control. Users wouldn't act on a ranking they couldn't interrogate. <span class="hl">Trust in an AI feature is designed, not inherited from the model quality.</span>`},
        {t:'What I would do differently',b:`I would instrument the product from day one. Almost every impact number on this page is a stakeholder estimate rather than a measurement, and that's a design failure as much as an analytics one — I didn't define success metrics before building, so I couldn't prove the thing I'd built worked.`}
      ].map((r,i)=>`
        <div class="rv" style="--i:${i}">
          <h4 class="h4" style="margin-bottom:12px">${r.t}</h4>
          <p class="body" style="font-size:.94rem">${r.b}</p>
        </div>`).join('')}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

${nextProject('hrm-genie')}
${foot()}`;
}
/* ============================================================
   CASE STUDY 02 — MDM GENIE
   ============================================================ */
function CS_MDM(){
  return `
<header class="cs-hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <a href="#/work" class="cs-back">${I.arL} All case studies</a>
    <p class="eyebrow rv" style="margin-bottom:20px"><span class="n">02</span> &nbsp;—&nbsp; Device management · 0→1 · Product Designer + Product Manager</p>
    <h1 class="h1 cs-title rv" style="--i:1">MDM Genie — <span class="em" style="color:var(--fg-2)">security you'd opt into</span></h1>
    <p class="lead cs-sub rv" style="--i:2">
      One interface governing Android, Windows and Apple fleets — 73 policies, thousands of endpoints,
      and a single wrong click capable of locking an entire company out of its laptops. I went in
      expecting a complexity problem. Research told me it was a trust problem, twice over.
    </p>
    <div class="cs-facts rv" style="--i:3">
      ${[
        {b:'Designer + PM',s:'Owned the policy roadmap and the design'},
        {b:'73 policies',s:'Architected across three OS platforms'},
        {b:'5 IT admins',s:'Embedded research across three companies'},
        {b:'42% → 79%',s:'BYOD enrolment willingness, moderated testing (n=5)'}
      ].map(f=>`<div class="cs-fact"><b>${f.b}</b><span>${f.s}</span></div>`).join('')}
    </div>
    ${roleNote(`Both roles again. As <strong>PM</strong> I owned the policy roadmap — deciding which of several hundred possible device controls made the first release, in what order, and which OS got parity first. As <strong>designer</strong> I did the research, the cross-OS information architecture, the policy schema and every screen.`,
      `<span class="hl">The 73-policy scope was a product decision before it was a design one.</span> Competitors ship 200+ and admins use a dozen. I cut to the set that mapped to real compliance requirements our customers were audited against, then designed each one to be configurable without a manual.`)}
    <div class="rv" style="--i:4">${ndaNote}</div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('01','The finding that redirected the project','The blocker wasn\'t usability. It was disbelief.')}
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="rv">
        <blockquote class="quote">
          <p>"I'm happy to enrol my phone for work. I just don't want IT seeing my personal photos or my WhatsApp."</p>
          <cite>Product designer, BYOD user — enrolment testing</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <div class="note" style="margin-top:28px">
          <span class="lbl">The reframe</span>
          <p>You cannot solve a trust problem with a usability solution. Employees weren't confused by the enrolment flow — they understood it perfectly and declined. Smoother onboarding would have moved nothing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stack-4 rv" style="--i:1">
        <p class="body">
          I started this project on the admin side, where the brief was: three separate tools for Android,
          Windows and Apple, inconsistent policy, no unified compliance view. That problem is real and
          I'll come back to it.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          <span class="hl">The brief was wrong, and research is what told me so.</span> I went in to validate
          an admin-side complexity hypothesis and came out with a finding that contradicted it.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          But BYOD enrolment was the bottleneck the business actually cared about, and it wasn't moving.
          My first enrolment flow was four clean steps, well under two minutes.
          <span class="hl">Stated willingness to enrol a personal device was 42%.</span>
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          The reason showed up in every session. Existing MDM tools are opaque about what they monitor,
          so employees assume the worst. The design problem wasn't friction —
          <span class="hl">it was that the product had no way to be believed.</span>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="method rv" style="margin-top:clamp(36px,5vw,60px)">
      <div class="method-head">
        <p class="eyebrow" style="margin:0">How I ran it</p>
        <p class="small" style="margin:0;color:var(--fg-4)">Method disclosed so you can weigh the findings yourself</p>
      </div>
      <div class="method-grid">
        ${[
          {k:'Recruitment',v:'IT managers and security analysts at five organisations, 200–2,000 employees, each managing at least two OS types. BYOD participants recruited separately — employees with a personal device enrolled or eligible.'},
          {k:'Protocol',v:'Contextual inquiry with admins — observed live in their existing tools rather than asking them to describe workflows. Semi-structured follow-up on policy configuration and incident response.'},
          {k:'Synthesis',v:'Affinity mapping across ~180 observations, clustered into pain themes, then policy-tree mapping to reconcile OS coverage differences into one schema.'},
          {k:'Evaluation',v:'Moderated usability testing with 5 IT admins on the shipped flows — task completion, error rates and cognitive load. BYOD enrolment tested separately with stated-willingness capture before and after.'}
        ].map(m=>`<div class="method-i"><b>${m.k}</b><p>${m.v}</p></div>`).join('')}
      </div>
      <div class="method-limit">
        <b>Limitations I'd want you to know</b>
        <p>Five admins is a small evaluative sample — enough to surface directional problems, not enough to size an effect precisely. The 42%→79% figure measures <em>stated</em> willingness, not enrolment behaviour, and stated intent overstates real adoption. I'd treat it as strong directional evidence that transparency was the blocker, not as a precise conversion number. A larger unmoderated study is what this needed and didn't get.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('02','Design decisions','Two decisions that defined the product')}

    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 01 · BYOD enrolment</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">I added a step to make the flow work</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        Standard practice says remove steps. I added one: an explicit, plain-language screen listing what
        IT can see and — critically — what it cannot, shown <em>before</em> the work profile is created rather
        than buried in a policy document afterwards.
      </p>
      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">v1 — optimised for speed</span>
          <p>Four steps, under two minutes: scan code, sign in, create work profile, apply policies. Clean, fast, conventional. 42% stated willingness to enrol.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">What testing surfaced</span>
          <p>Participants didn't fail any task. They completed the flow and then said they wouldn't do it with their real phone. Task success and adoption were measuring different things.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat good">
          <span class="lbl">v2 — optimised for belief</span>
          <p>A fifth step: two explicit columns, <strong>"What IT can see"</strong> and <strong>"What IT can never see"</strong>, in plain language, before enrolment completes. Willingness moved to 79%.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="note" style="margin-bottom:30px">
        <span class="lbl">The principle I took from this</span>
        <p>Friction isn't inherently bad — unexplained friction is. A step that answers the question blocking the user is not a cost, it's the entire product. And "task completion" as a usability metric can be actively misleading when the real question is whether someone would <em>choose</em> to do the task at all.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel rv">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">MDM Genie — enrolment, step 3 of 5</span>
          <span class="live"><i></i> Recreation</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <p class="h4" style="margin-bottom:6px">Before you continue — here's exactly what this does</p>
          <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:22px;max-width:52ch">Your work profile is separate from your personal one. This is the full list. Nothing is omitted.</p>
          <div class="grid-2 tight">
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--warn);margin-bottom:12px">What IT can see</p>
              <div class="trust">
                ${['Apps installed in your work profile','Work profile security settings','Device model and OS version','Whether disk encryption is on','Corporate network connection status']
                  .map(t=>`<div class="trust-r"><span class="trust-ic" style="background:var(--warn-bg);color:var(--warn)">●</span><p>${t}</p></div>`).join('')}
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--ok);margin-bottom:12px">What IT can never see</p>
              <div class="trust">
                ${['Personal photos, videos or files','Personal messages, WhatsApp, SMS','Browsing history outside work apps','Personal app usage or activity','Your location, at any time']
                  .map(t=>`<div class="trust-r"><span class="trust-ic" style="background:var(--ok-bg);color:var(--ok)">✓</span><p>${t}</p></div>`).join('')}
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="callout" style="margin-top:22px;font-size:.82rem">
            You can unenrol at any time from Settings. Your personal data is never touched — removing the work profile deletes only work data.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 02 · Policy architecture</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">One policy form, three operating systems</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        73 policies. Some are universal (password length). Some are OS-specific (BitLocker on Windows,
        AirDrop on Apple, factory-reset protection on Android). Competitors solve this by shipping three
        separate configuration surfaces, which is why policy drifts between platforms.
      </p>
      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">Rejected — tabbed by OS</span>
          <p>My first architecture. Clean to build, and it recreates the exact problem we were replacing: the admin still has to configure the same intent three times and hope they match.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">Rejected — show everything</span>
          <p>One flat list of all 73. Honest, and paralysing. Admins in testing couldn't distinguish the four settings that mattered from the sixty-nine that didn't.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat good">
          <span class="lbl">Shipped — progressive disclosure by relevance</span>
          <p>One form. Select target platforms and irrelevant policies visibly fade rather than disappear — so the admin learns the coverage model instead of just being protected from it. Complexity available, not constantly visible.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="panel rv" id="demo-policy-builder">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">MDM Genie — cross-platform policy builder</span>
          <span class="live"><i></i> Interactive</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <p class="small" style="margin-bottom:16px">Toggle target platforms — watch which policies stay applicable.</p>
          <div class="os-tabs" id="os-tabs" role="group" aria-label="Target platforms"></div>
          <div class="pol" id="pol-list"></div>
          <div style="display:flex;gap:26px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-top:22px;padding-top:20px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
            <div class="work-stat"><b id="pb-applicable" style="color:var(--ok)">—</b><span>policies applicable</span></div>
            <div class="work-stat"><b id="pb-enabled">—</b><span>enabled in this profile</span></div>
            <div class="work-stat"><b id="pb-os">—</b><span>platforms targeted</span></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="small rv" style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--fg-4);font-size:.76rem">
        Showing 14 of 73 policies. Fade rather than hide was a deliberate choice — admins told us disappearing
        options made them distrust whether a setting had been applied.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- DECISION 3 — DESTRUCTIVE ACTIONS -->
    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 03 · Destructive actions</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">The most important screen in the product is the one that slows you down</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        MDM Genie can remotely wipe a device, revoke access, force a password reset, or push a policy to
        an entire fleet at once. These are the features IT buys the product for.
        <span class="hl">They are also the ones that end careers.</span>
      </p>
      <p class="body read" style="margin-top:14px">
        One admin described the incident that had happened at his previous company: a policy targeting
        the wrong device group locked several hundred employees out of their laptops on a Monday morning.
        The tool did exactly what it was told. Nothing in the interface had made the blast radius visible
        before the click.
      </p>

      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">Industry default</span>
          <p>A confirmation modal reading "Are you sure? This action cannot be undone." Users click through these reflexively — it's a liability shield, not a safety mechanism.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">Why it fails</span>
          <p>It asks for confirmation without providing information. The admin already believed they were sure. What they needed was to <em>see</em> that their filter caught 340 devices instead of 4.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat good">
          <span class="lbl">What shipped — three layers</span>
          <p>A blast-radius preview naming the actual devices and owners. Typed confirmation proportional to severity. And a live undo window before the command dispatches — recovery, not just prevention.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="note" style="margin-bottom:30px">
        <span class="lbl">The principle</span>
        <p>Friction should be proportional to consequence, and it should be <em>informative</em> friction. A modal that makes you pause without telling you anything is theatre. A preview that shows you 340 names when you expected 4 is the entire safety mechanism — <span class="hl">the pause exists to deliver the information, not to slow you down.</span></p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel rv" id="demo-wipe">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">MDM Genie — remote action</span>
          <span class="live"><i></i> Interactive</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <div class="grid-2 tight">
            <div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:14px">Action</p>
              <div class="acts" id="acts"></div>
              <p class="eyebrow" style="margin:24px 0 12px">Target group</p>
              <select id="wipe-target" class="sel" style="width:100%">
                <option value="one">Single device — Dell XPS (Tom V.)</option>
                <option value="team">Field Team — 12 devices</option>
                <option value="all">All Windows devices — 340 devices</option>
              </select>
            </div>
            <div id="wipe-panel"></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="small rv" style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--fg-4);font-size:.76rem">
        Try <strong style="color:var(--fg-3)">Remote wipe</strong> on <strong style="color:var(--fg-3)">All Windows devices</strong> — the confirmation requirement scales with the blast radius, and the undo window is real.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- DECISION 4 — COMPLIANCE MONITOR -->
    <div class="dec rv">
      <span class="dec-n">Decision 04 · Compliance monitoring</span>
      <h3 class="h3 dec-t">A dashboard that shows everything tells you nothing</h3>
      <p class="body read">
        Research finding, verbatim from every admin I spoke to: policy violations were discovered through
        incidents, not monitoring. Proactive alerting was the single most requested capability across all
        five interviews. So the compliance monitor was the highest-priority item on the roadmap —
        which made getting it wrong the first time expensive.
      </p>

      <div class="beats">
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">v1 — complete visibility</span>
          <p>Every device, every policy, every status, filterable. Comprehensive and genuinely accurate. In testing, admins opened it, scrolled, and closed it without acting on anything.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat bad">
          <span class="lbl">The diagnosis</span>
          <p>I'd designed for the question "what is the state of my fleet?" The question admins actually arrive with is <em>"what do I need to do right now?"</em> Those need almost opposite interfaces.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="beat good">
          <span class="lbl">v2 — triage first, browse second</span>
          <p>Severity-ranked queue as the default view: what's critical, what it affects, what the remediation is. Full fleet state still one click away — but no longer the thing that greets you.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="note" style="margin-bottom:30px">
        <span class="lbl">What I'd generalise</span>
        <p>"Give users all the data and let them decide" sounds respectful and is often an abdication. Deciding what deserves attention <em>is</em> the design work in any monitoring product. <span class="hl">If everything is visible, the tool has quietly handed the prioritisation problem back to the user</span> — which is the problem they came to it with.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel rv" id="demo-comp">
        <div class="panel-bar">
          <span class="dots"><i></i><i></i><i></i></span>
          <span class="panel-title">MDM Genie — compliance monitor</span>
          <span class="live"><i></i> Interactive</span>
        </div>
        <div class="panel-body">
          <div class="fleet">
            ${[['Android','97.2','var(--ok)'],['Windows','88.4','var(--warn)'],['Apple','94.1','var(--ok)']].map(([n,v,c])=>`
              <div class="fleet-i">
                <div class="fleet-t"><span>${n}</span><b style="color:${c}">${v}%</b></div>
                <span class="comp-tr"><span class="comp-f" style="width:${v}%;background:${c}"></span></span>
              </div>`).join('')}
            <div class="fleet-i overall">
              <div class="fleet-t"><span>Fleet overall</span><b>92.7%</b></div>
              <span class="comp-tr"><span class="comp-f" style="width:92.7%;background:var(--fg-2)"></span></span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="triage-bar">
            <p class="eyebrow" style="margin:0">Needs attention</p>
            <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Severity filter" id="sev-seg">
              <button data-sev="all" aria-pressed="true">All</button>
              <button data-sev="critical" aria-pressed="false">Critical</button>
              <button data-sev="warning" aria-pressed="false">Warning</button>
              <button data-sev="info" aria-pressed="false">Info</button>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="alerts" id="alerts"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('04','System scope','The rest of the surface','Four decisions carry the narrative. This is the rest of what shipped — included so you can judge the scope, not the storytelling.')}
    <div class="scope rv">
      ${[
        {t:'Zero-touch enrolment',d:'Devices provisioned and policy-configured without IT physically touching them'},
        {t:'BYOD work profiles',d:'Container separation with explicit, visible boundaries between work and personal'},
        {t:'Kiosk mode',d:'Single-purpose device lockdown for shop floor and field deployments'},
        {t:'Application management',d:'Force-install, blocklists, managed Play Store, per-OS app policy'},
        {t:'Policy conflict resolution',d:'Detects contradictory policies across overlapping device groups before rollout'},
        {t:'Rollout scheduling',d:'Staged deployment with per-wave monitoring instead of fleet-wide pushes'},
        {t:'Audit logs',d:'Every action timestamped and attributed — reconstructable for security audits and GDPR'},
        {t:'Remote actions',d:'Lock, wipe, locate, password reset, with the safety model shown above'},
        {t:'Device inventory',d:'Fleet-wide search, grouping, ownership and lifecycle state'},
        {t:'Compliance reporting',d:'Exportable posture reports mapped to the frameworks customers are audited against'}
      ].map(s=>`<div class="scope-i"><b>${s.t}</b><span>${s.d}</span></div>`).join('')}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('05','Architecture','Four layers, because the translation has to live somewhere','The product\'s real job is translating one human-readable intent into three different OS-specific enforcement commands. That translation layer is the product.')}
    <div class="stack-3 rv">
      ${[
        {l:'Layer 01 — Admin interface',d:'Unified dashboard · Policy builder · Device inventory · Compliance monitor · Remote actions',c:'var(--ac)'},
        {l:'Layer 02 — Policy engine',d:'Validator · Conflict resolver · Rollout scheduler · Compliance engine · Alerting',c:'var(--fg-2)'},
        {l:'Layer 03 — OS connectors',d:'Android EMM API · Windows MDM / Intune · Apple MDM protocol',c:'var(--fg-3)'},
        {l:'Layer 04 — Device fleet',d:'Corporate-owned · BYOD enrolled · Kiosk mode · Zero-touch provisioned',c:'var(--fg-4)'}
      ].map((r,i)=>`
        <div style="border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:2px solid ${r.c};border-radius:0 var(--r) var(--r) 0;padding:16px 20px;background:var(--bg-1)">
          <p class="small mono" style="font-size:.68rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:${r.c};margin-bottom:6px">${r.l}</p>
          <p class="body" style="font-size:.9rem;color:var(--fg-2)">${r.d}</p>
        </div>`).join('')}
    </div>
    <div class="note rv" style="margin-top:26px">
      <span class="lbl">Why a designer drew this</span>
      <p>Because layer 2 is where every meaningful UX decision gets made or lost. Conflict resolution, rollout staging and the shape of a compliance alert are interface problems that happen to be expressed as system architecture. If I'd waited for engineering to define this layer, I'd have been designing screens over decisions I didn't make.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('06','Outcomes','What shipped')}
    <div class="mets rv">
      ${[
        {b:'42% → 79%',l:'BYOD enrolment willingness',m:'Moderated usability testing, n=5, stated willingness before and after the transparency step'},
        {b:'73 policies',l:'Architected across Android, Windows and Apple',m:'Countable design artifact — the full policy matrix I specified'},
        {b:'3 → 1',l:'Tools consolidated into one interface',m:'Replaced separate Android EMM, Intune and Apple MDM administration'},
        {b:'Under 5 min',l:'Zero-touch enrolment, no IT contact',m:'Measured in testing on the shipped flow'}
      ].map(m=>`<div class="met"><b>${m.b}</b><span class="ml">${m.l}</span><span class="mm">${m.m}</span></div>`).join('')}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="rule">

<section class="sect">
  <div class="wrap">
    ${sectionHead('07','Reflection','What this taught me')}
    <div class="grid-2">
      ${[
        {t:'High-stakes interfaces need deliberate friction',b:`A misconfigured policy can lock 2,000 people out of their laptops. Every destructive action here has a confirmation, a preview of exactly which devices are affected, and an undo window. That's not poor UX — <span class="hl">low friction is a value, not a law,</span> and irreversibility is when you trade it away.`},
        {t:'B2B products have two users with opposing interests',b:`The admin wants control and visibility. The employee wants privacy and autonomy. These are in genuine tension and cannot both be maximised. Most MDM tools resolve it by serving the buyer and ignoring the user — which is precisely why adoption stalls.`},
        {t:'Task success can hide the real failure',b:`Every participant completed my v1 enrolment flow. Every metric said it worked. They just wouldn't have done it voluntarily. I now ask "would you actually do this?" separately from "can you do this?" — they're different questions and only one predicts adoption.`},
        {t:'What I would do differently',b:`n=5 is too small to carry the 42→79% claim as confidently as I'd like. It was directionally unambiguous and every participant gave the same reasoning, but I should have run a larger unmoderated study to firm it up. I'm stating the sample size here rather than quoting the number bare.`}
      ].map((r,i)=>`
        <div class="rv" style="--i:${i}">
          <h4 class="h4" style="margin-bottom:12px">${r.t}</h4>
          <p class="body" style="font-size:.94rem">${r.b}</p>
        </div>`).join('')}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

${nextProject('mdm-genie')}
${foot()}`;
}
